    // Missing call to io_queue_linked_timeout(linked_timeout);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when `io_prep_linked_timeout()` is called, it prepares a linked timeout but does not subsequently call `io_queue_linked_timeout()` in the punting path when the request cannot be issued immediately. This results in linked timeouts being prepared but never queued, leading to issues in managing timeout events.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!io_arm_poll_handler(req)) {
    ret = io_prep_work_files(req);
    if (unlikely(ret))
        goto err;
    io_queue_async_work(req);
}
if (linked_timeout)
    io_queue_linked_timeout(linked_timeout);
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that `io_queue_linked_timeout(linked_timeout)` is called after the async work is prepared, regardless of whether the polling handler was armed or not. This guarantees that the linked timeouts are queued properly, thus resolving the issue of prepared timeouts not being managed correctly.

